{-
   Emptying a `&` and not filling it again, in the build that could not see it.

   One rule, `doc/spec/ownership.md`'s: ownership may be taken out of storage reached through an
   exclusive borrow when every path from there to the point the frame gives the borrow up writes a
   value back. `Reject.Ownership.yana` is where the rule is stated over a concrete body; this is the
   same rule over an erased one, and until `eachEmptiedPlace` there was no such thing.

   What made them differ was the shape of the hand-over rather than anything about the rule.
   `sinkValue` puts an `InstMove` in front of a `->` argument, and the borrow lattice's interning
   scan, its forward walk and `checkMoves` all asked `kind == Value::Move`. A deferred class dispatch
   has no move to ask about - `emitGenericDispatch` applies no conventions, the callee not being a
   function that call site reaches - so `eat(s)` below was **rejected specialized and accepted
   generic**, and which one a program got was an optimization decision.

   The `.generic` marker is the point of the fixture: with specialization declined this body is the
   only one built, so nothing but the erased check can produce the diagnostic below.
-}
class Eat(a):
  fn eat(->x: a) -> {}

instance Eat(String):
    fn eat(->x: String) -> {} = {}

-- The rejection. `s` is emptied by the hand-over and no path writes anything back into it.
fn (Eat(a)) drain(&s: a) -> {} = eat(s)

{-
   And the control, which is the same hand-over with the write-back the rule asks for - `++=`'s
   body, one class deeper. It has to be here rather than only in `test/lib`: a check that reports
   nothing is indistinguishable from a check that is not running unless something beside it fires.
-}
class Refill(a):
  fn refilled(->x: a) -> a

instance Refill(String):
    fn refilled(->x: String) -> String = x

fn (Refill(a)) cycle(&s: a) -> {}:
    s = refilled(s)

fn main() -> Int = 0
